 ### Normal technology and resilience
 
 **Best case.** AI is a normal technology: its impacts materialize not as capabilities improve but as they are diffused through the economy, at the pace of institutional adoption, so the danger to govern is that of a powerful general-purpose technology, and policy should build resilience and reject nonproliferation, which lowers it (Narayanan and Kapoor 2025). In the history of general-purpose technologies, great-power advantage has come from the capacity to diffuse them, not from a lead at the frontier (Ding 2024). On this reading no lead at the frontier is decisive in itself, and custody of a decisive class is a concentration of power answering a danger that does not arise.
 
-**What remains.** The growth channel of Section 5.3 binds at the pace of deployment, not of the frontier: where adoption is slow enough for contestable coverage to keep up, (coverage lags) fails for that class, and where it is not, the rate of deployment is the rate to compare with coverage; both are measured class by class (C1). Whether any advantage is decisive is C5. The diagnosis registers both, and where both are measured favourable the activation of tier 2 lapses (THEORY §12.3) while the requirements on correction, coverage and measurement stand. The concentration that custody risks is answered by its form, separately held functions certified by row sums, and by the guardian test ({{twoorders}}; {{guardian}}). **What it changes.** Its prescriptions, resilience, diffusion of what is not decisive and shared measurement, are G1, G3 and G6, and diffusion is a lever of Part I in every channel ({{chansign}}); it does not change the selection of those who would lead where leads do compound. **What would show it suffices:** contestable coverage keeping pace with deployment class by class (C1), and leads that persist on no measure, with others balancing a rising actor (C5).
